WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.800
Network and transport-layer controls are meant to reduce unwanted traffic pressure before

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.700
it damages availability.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.560
At a defensive level, these controls may include rate limits, connection limits,

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.380
SYN protection, upstream filtering, traffic shaping,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.760
allowlists for critical administration, and carefully scoped blocklists.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.940
The exact implementation depends on the provider, platform, network architecture,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.760
and service requirements.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:32.780
This course does not provide packet-level recipes or firewall command lines because those

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
details can be misused and are also highly environment-specific.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.140
The operational principle is scope.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.640
A control that is too narrow may not reduce pressure.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.820
A control that is too broad may block legitimate users, partners, monitoring systems,

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:53.320
provider health checks, payment integrations, support tools, or recovery access.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.260
Geo or ASN-based decisions can sometimes reduce obvious unwanted pressure,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.520
but they can also exclude real customers or business partners.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.540
Defenders should understand the business impact before applying controls that affect broad

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:07.880
populations.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.580
Upstream filtering can be especially valuable because local capacity may already be

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.600
saturated by the time traffic reaches the organization.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.300
If the provider can drop or shape traffic earlier,

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.080
the protected environment may recover without every local device processing every packet

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.420
or connection.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.680
That requires provider engagement, evidence,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.140
and a clear description of the observed impact.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.880
It also requires trust in the provider's control plane and a documented path to reverse or

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.460
adjust the action.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.120
Every emergency network control should be observable and reversible.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.700
Before and after applying a control, track the signal it is expected to change: bandwidth,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.360
connection rate, latency, errors, saturation, or successful user traffic.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:56.500
Record who approved the change, when it was applied, what it affected,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.320
and how rollback will happen.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.580
The best network mitigation is not the most aggressive block.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.280
It is the smallest effective control that protects critical availability while preserving

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.940
legitimate access.
